From c13a40571876b239e10953be1383132d9151f460 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Sugiyama <yuichis@ricsec.co.jp>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:40:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] pta: veraison_attestation: report the TEE core as a
 software component

The evidence so far describes only the calling TA. Add a second
software component for the TEE core itself, measurement-type "PRoT",
so that a verifier can tell which OP-TEE build the TA runs on and
reject known-bad or outdated builds that still pass secure boot.

The measurement-value is the SHA-256 of the core's .text and .rodata,
taken over the same ranges as the attestation PTA
(core/pta/attestation.c) so the two agree and the value can be
computed from tee.elf ahead of time. The relocated read-only data is
not included, which keeps the value independent of the load address.
The version field (claim 4) carries TEE_IMPL_VERSION, the first word
of the core version string, so the build is identifiable even where no
reference value is provisioned. The signer-id is the boot signer
already reported for early TAs: the SRK hash with the platform claims
on i.MX 8M, a fixed test value otherwise.

cbor.c takes the components as an array and emits the optional version
field; the signing paths are unchanged.

This is a self-measurement: a compromised core could report any value.
The component identifies a genuine build under the assumption, stated
in the README, that secure boot only starts genuine ones.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Sugiyama <yuichis@ricsec.co.jp>
